#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085} :activity/id #uuid "2334b80e-a2d6-550a-8293-e10100602247" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050783 :topic/id #uuid "66b05ba4-f638-5531-98d7-9a7817e5a7aa" :topic/math-academy-id 282} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065435 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2dcb6a2-e8f7-5bb7-9450-e0b65532681a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 867 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa63ec49-ee39-504e-babd-8b90d77dcaf3" :step/math-academy-id 1675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064093 :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186